Presidential elections were held in [[São Tomé and Príncipe]] on 18 July 2021.<ref name=":0">{{Cite web|last=Carlos|first=Maximino|date=12 June 2021|title=Mais de uma dezena de candidatos às eleições presidenciais sãotomenses|url=https://www.rfi.fr/pt/s%C3%A3o-tom%C3%A9-e-pr%C3%ADncipe/20210612-mais-de-uma-dezena-de-candidatos-%C3%A0s-elei%C3%A7%C3%B5es-presidenciais-s%C3%A3otomenses|url-status=live|access-date=2 July 2021|website=RFI|language=pt|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210613031919/https://www.rfi.fr/pt/s%C3%A3o-tom%C3%A9-e-pr%C3%ADncipe/20210612-mais-de-uma-dezena-de-candidatos-%C3%A0s-elei%C3%A7%C3%B5es-presidenciais-s%C3%A3otomenses |archive-date=13 June 2021 }}</ref>  As no presidential candidate received a majority of the vote, a second round was originally scheduled to be held on 8 August 2021.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Musambi|first=Evelyne|last2=Rukanga|first2=Basillioh|date=19 July 2021|title=Africa Live: Sao Tome presidential election heads for a runoff|url=https://www.bbc.com/news/live/world-africa-47639452?pinned_post_locator=urn:asset:81a8f4d6-a458-44c9-8a59-b9e2318e4634|url-status=live|access-date=20 July 2021|website=BBC News|language=en-gb|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210720111945/https://www.bbc.com/news/live/world-africa-47639452?pinned_post_locator=urn:asset:81a8f4d6-a458-44c9-8a59-b9e2318e4634 |archive-date=20 July 2021 }}</ref> However, following an objection to the first-round result, the second round was postponed to 29 August 2021, and later postponed again to 5 September 2021.<ref>{{Cite web|date=August 7, 2021|title=São Tomé-et-Principe: le second tour de l'élection présidentielle repoussé au 29 août|url=https://www.rfi.fr/fr/afrique/20210807-s%C3%A3o-tom%C3%A9-et-principe-le-second-tour-de-l-%C3%A9lection-pr%C3%A9sidentielle-repouss%C3%A9-au-29-ao%C3%BBt|url-status=live|access-date=August 9, 2021|website=RFI|language=fr|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210807024228/https://www.rfi.fr/fr/afrique/20210807-s%C3%A3o-tom%C3%A9-et-principe-le-second-tour-de-l-%C3%A9lection-pr%C3%A9sidentielle-repouss%C3%A9-au-29-ao%C3%BBt |archive-date=7 August 2021 }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=Le second tour de la présidentielle à Sao Tomé-et-Principe de nouveau repoussé |url=https://www.voaafrique.com/a/le-second-tour-de-la-pr%C3%A9sidentielle-%C3%A0-sao-tom%C3%A9-et-principe-de-nouveau-repouss%C3%A9/6006928.html |website=VOA |access-date=20 August 2021 |language=fr}}</ref>

The second round was won by [[Carlos Vila Nova]] of [[Independent Democratic Action]], who received 58% of the vote, defeating [[Guilherme Posser da Costa]] of the [[Movement for the Liberation of São Tomé and Príncipe – Social Democratic Party|MLSTP–PSD]].<ref name="obs">[https://observador.pt/2021/09/06/sao-tome-carlos-vila-nova-vence-presidenciais-com-5754-dos-votos/ São Tomé: Carlos Vila Nova vence presidenciais com 57,54% dos votos] Observador, 6 September 2021</ref> Turnout for the second round was around 65%.<ref name="anews">{{Cite web|title=Opposition candidate Carlos Vila Nova wins Sao Tome presidency: partial results|url=https://www.africanews.com/2021/09/06/opposition-candidate-carlos-vila-nova-wins-sao-tome-presidency-partial/|url-status=live|access-date=2021-09-06|website=Africanews|language=en|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210906150425/https://www.africanews.com/2021/09/06/opposition-candidate-carlos-vila-nova-wins-sao-tome-presidency-partial/ |archive-date=6 September 2021 }}</ref>

==Electoral system==
The [[President of São Tomé and Príncipe]] is elected using the [[two-round system]] for a five-year term. If no candidate receives more than 50% of the vote in the first round of voting, a run-off is held with the top two candidates.<ref>{{cite web |title=Country Profile: Sao Tome and Principe |url=https://www.electionguide.org/countries/id/188/ |website=IFES Election Guide |access-date=1 February 2021}}</ref> The role of president is largely ceremonial, with power in the hands of the prime minister.<ref name="guardianng">{{Cite news|url=https://guardian.ng/news/sao-tome-holds-postponed-presidential-election/|location=Lagos, Nigeria|title=Sao Tome holds postponed presidential election|date=5 September 2021|access-date=8 September 2021|newspaper=[[The Guardian (Nigeria)|The Guardian]]}}</ref>

A total of 123,302 voters were registered,<ref name=DW /> of which 14,693 were abroad, including 7,378 in Portugal.<ref name=OBS />

==Candidates==
Incumbent president [[Evaristo Carvalho]] did not seek re-election.<ref name=":0" /> Former prime minister [[Patrice Trovoada]] had been mentioned as a potential candidate, despite corruption allegations;<ref name="ACSS">{{cite web|title=Elections in Africa in 2021|url=https://africacenter.org/spotlight/2021-elections/|access-date=1 February 2021|website=Africa Center for Strategic Studies}}</ref> he also did not file as a candidate.<ref name=":0" />

[[Elsa Garrido]], president of the [[Social Democratic Movement – Green Party of São Tomé and Príncipe|Social Democratic Movement – Green Party]] announced her candidacy on 23 December 2020.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Neto |first1=Ricardo |title=Elsa Garrido, líder do Partido Verde anuncia candidatura as eleições presidenciais de 2021 |url=http://www.stp-press.st/2020/12/23/elsa-garrido-lider-do-partido-verde-anuncia-candidatura-as-eleicoes-presidenciais-de-2021/ |website=STP-PRESS |access-date=1 February 2021 |language=pt-PT |date=23 December 2020}}</ref>

In total, 19 candidates filed to run.<ref name=":0" /><ref name="Candidats Tela Non">{{cite web|title=Corte Constitucional confirmou 19 candidatos para as eleições presidenciais|url=https://www.telanon.info/politica/2021/06/11/34495/corte-constitucional-confirmou-19-candidatos-para-as-eleicoes-presidenciais/|author=Abel Veiga|website=Téla Nón|language=pt|date=11 June 2021|access-date=12 June 2021|publication-date=}}.</ref>
