==Campaign==
For the first round, the campaign took place in the two weeks prior to election day.<ref name="DW">{{Cite journal|url=https://www.dw.com/pt-002/19-candidatos-disputam-presidenciais-mais-concorridas-de-sempre-em-são-tomé/a-58288197|website=[[Deutsche Welle]]|title=19 candidatos disputam presidenciais mais concorridas de sempre em São Tomé|date=16 July 2021|first=Ramusel|last=Graça}}</ref> Most of the candidates denounced corruption in the country. In his 12 July national holiday speech, outgoing president Evaristo Carvalho denounced the practice of "bathing" ("banho"), or buying votes, as "exploitation of the poverty of citizens.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.rfi.fr/fr/afrique/20210718-présidentielle-à-sao-tomé-et-principe-les-habitants-appelés-aux-urnes|website=[[Radio France Internationale]]|title=Présidentielle à Sao Tomé-et-Principe: les habitants appelés aux urnes|date=18 July 2021}}</ref><ref name="OBS">{{Cite web|url=https://observador.pt/2021/07/16/eleicoes-em-sao-tome-e-principe-termina-esta-sexta-feira-campanha-de-19-candidatos-as-presidenciais/|website=[[Observador]]|title=Eleições em São Tomé e Príncipe. Termina esta sexta-feira campanha de 19 candidatos às presidenciais|date=16 July 2021}}</ref> The Union for Progress and Change in Principe, the majority party in the [[Príncipe]] island assembly since its founding in 2006, did not support a candidate.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.stp-press.st/2021/06/13/umpp-da-liberdade-de-voto-aos-seus-militantes-nas-proximas-eleicoes-presidenciais/|website=Agência STP-Press|title=UMPP dá liberdade de voto aos seus militantes nas próximas Eleições Presidenciais|first=Manuel|last=Dênde|date=13 June 2021}}</ref>

The second round was delayed while the courts considered a petition filed by third-place candidate Delfim Neves alleging fraud in the first round, which was ultimately rejected.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.monitor.co.ug/uganda/news/opposition-candidate-wins-sao-tome-presidency-3541000|website=[[Daily Monitor]]|title=Opposition candidate wins Sao Tome presidency|date=7 September 2021}}</ref> The campaign for the second round began on 26 August and lasted ten days plus. On the first day of the campaign, Minister of Health Edgar Neves warned of the exponential increase in the number of cases of COVID-19 and that a third wave of the pandemic was beginning in the country.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.voaportugues.com/a/arranca-a-campanha-eleitoral-para-a-segunda-volta-das-presidenciais-em-são-tomé-e-pr%C3%ADncipe/6016835.html|website=[[Voice of America]] in Portuguese|title=Arranca a campanha eleitoral para a segunda volta das Presidenciais em São Tomé e Príncipe|first=Óscar|last=Medeiros|date=26 August 2021}}</ref>

==Conduct==
According to the president of the National Electoral Commission, Fernando Maquengo, the campaign took place in an atmosphere of peace and tranquility.<ref name="RFI">{{Cite web|url=https://www.rfi.fr/fr/afrique/20210717-fin-de-la-campagne-électorale-à-sao-tomé-et-principe-la-corruption-dénoncée-à-tous-les-niveaux|website=[[Radio France Internationale]]|title=Fin de la campagne électorale à Sao Tomé-et-Principe: la corruption dénoncée à tous les niveaux|date=17 July 2021}}</ref> The election was marked by protective measures related to the [[COVID-19 pandemic in São Tomé and Príncipe|COVID-19 pandemic]].<ref name=DW /> Gatherings were forbidden and social distancing was mandatory,<ref name=DW /> instructions that were not always respected by the candidates.<ref name= RFI />

Four election observation missions were sent, from the African Union, the Economic Community of Central African States (including representatives from Angola, Guinea-Bissau, Mali, and Cameroon)<ref name=OOBBSS/> the United States, and Japan.<ref name=DW /> In a preliminary report, [[African Union]] observers praised the "peaceful and calm environment" in which the second round took place.<ref>{{cite web|date=8 September 2021|title=Sao Tome: African Union observers hail ‘peaceful, calm’ presidential elections|url=https://www.macaubusiness.com/sao-tome-african-union-observers-hail-peaceful-calm-presidential-elections/|access-date=8 September 2021|work=macaubusiness.com}}</ref>

Voting took place at 304 polling stations,<ref name="OOBBSS">{{Cite web|url=https://observador.pt/2021/07/17/sao-tome-comissao-eleitoral-garante-reforco-policial-para-evitar-incidentes-na-divulgacao-de-resultados/|website=Observador|title=São Tomé. Comissão Eleitoral garante reforço policial para evitar incidentes na divulgação de resultados|date=17 July 2021}}</ref> with 42 polling stations for overseas voters.<ref name=OOBBSS/>

==Results==
The second round was won by [[Carlos Vila Nova]] of the country's largest opposition party, [[Independent Democratic Action]], who received 58% of the vote, defeating [[Guilherme Posser da Costa]] of the ruling [[Movement for the Liberation of São Tomé and Príncipe – Social Democratic Party|MLSTP–PSD]].<ref name=obs/> Turnout for the second round was around 65%.<ref name=anews /> The runoff was required after neither candidate reached 50% of the vote in the first round on 18 July, with Vila Nova receiving 39.5% and Posser da Costa 20.7%.<ref name="guardianng"/>

