== Aftermath ==
Vila Nova said, "With the announcement of the preliminary results, I won those elections in a clear way and this result allows me to be considered as the elected President."<ref>{{cite web|date=7 September 2021|title=ADVERTISEMENT  The East AfricanRest Of Africa Opposition's Carlos Vila Nova becomes Sao Tome's new President|url=https://www.theeastafrican.co.ke/tea/rest-of-africa/sao-tome-new-president-3540990|access-date=8 September 2021|work=The East African}}{{Dead link|date=September 2026 |bot=InternetArchiveBot }}</ref>

The prime minister of Sao Tome and Principe, [[Jorge Bom Jesus]], congratulated the President-elect, Carlos Vila Nova, on his victory in the second round of the presidential election last Sunday and said he was available for “all collaboration and loyalty”.<ref>{{Cite web|date=2021-09-09|title=Sao Tome: PM congratulates president-elect, promises collaboration|url=https://www.macaubusiness.com/sao-tome-pm-congratulates-president-elect-promises-collaboration-loyalty/|access-date=2021-09-12|website=Macau Business|language=en-GB}}</ref>

Vila Nova was also congratulated by the [[Angola]]n president [[João Lourenço]],<ref>{{cite web|date=7 September 2021|title=Angolan President Congratulates São Tomé & Príncipe President-Elect|url=https://allafrica.com/stories/202109080493.html|access-date=8 September 2021|work=Angola Press Agence/allafrica.com}}</ref> and the [[President of Portugal|Portuguese president]] [[Marcelo Rebelo de Sousa]].<ref>{{cite web|title=Marcelo saúda Presidente eleito de São Tomé e tenciona estar na sua tomada de posse|url=https://observador.pt/2021/09/06/marcelo-sauda-presidente-eleito-de-sao-tome-e-tenciona-estar-na-sua-tomada-de-posse/|date=6 September 2021|work=Observador}}</ref>

